   "analysis": {
    "headline": "Footwear’s 25.3% return-request rate is above apparel’s, with sizing and fit the strongest reported associations.",
    "findings": [
     "Footwear had 1,860 return requests against 7,349 delivered lines: 25.3%, versus 23.1% for apparel and 7.7% for accessories. The footwear rate was 2.2 percentage points above apparel.",
     "Lines marked Too Large and Too Small had return rates of 59.3% and 58.0%, versus 18.8% for Fits. This is a strong fit association, though not proof that fit caused the returns.",
     "Size Issue accounted for 435 requests and Fit Issue for 266: together, 37.7% of footwear requests. Quality Issue was also prominent at 380 requests, or 20.4%.",
     "Casual Shoes generated the most requests, at 595, despite a 24.8% rate. Formal Shoes had the highest segment rate at 27.2%, but on just 327 delivered lines."
    ],
    "recommendations": [
     "Review footwear size guides and product measurements, prioritising Casual Shoes because of their high return volume.",
     "Investigate the 380 quality-related requests to identify recurring product or supplier issues.",
     "Examine sizing outcomes and return reasons within footwear articles before testing changes to product guidance."
    ],
    "caveats": [
     "The cohort covers orders placed from 1 January to 24 October 2026; some may still be in transit or awaiting return requests.",
     "The rate divides all return-requested sale lines by delivered sale lines; the SQL does not explicitly require requested lines to be delivered.",
     "Fit and reported reasons are associations, not proof of cause. The data is simulated."
